      "decision_id": "dec-isp-002",
      "question": "Name the accountable production deployment owner and the runtime host for the independent Dotmac ISP assembly. TWO hosts have now been offered and both are REFUSED as stated, for the same reason: each is already carrying live production traffic for a different system. (1) `selfcare.dotmac.io` (2026-08-21) is the live dotmac_sub production host (vmi3348415) and the compiled-in default API base URL of shipped field-mobile store builds, so it names the system being replaced rather than an independent runtime. (2) `158.220.82.88` (2026-08-22) is `ns3.dotmac.ng` (vmi3519229), a live PowerDNS authoritative secondary and one of three nodes serving the `dotmac.ng` zone; placing an application runtime or a test database on it puts DNS resolution for the whole estate behind an unrelated workload. Answer two things separately: which host runs asm-dotmac-isp and its own database — it must be a host with no existing production role — and whether `selfcare.dotmac.io` is intended as the eventual cutover destination for customer traffic, which would repoint every installed mobile client and is a client-compatibility commitment, not a DNS change.",
      "owner": "Michael Ayoade",
      "resolution": "SUPERSEDED, not answered. The question presumed a separate ISP runtime needing its own host, database and deployment owner. Dotmac Sub is not being retired: it remains the ISP product, runtime, API identity, hostname and customer-facing application, and becomes a thin assembly in place. No separate host, second production database, mobile API-base repoint or cross-database sealing protocol is required, so host procurement stops being a programme gate. Three hosts had been offered and each already carried production; that search is closed rather than won.",
